# renki-dirs. Design

The table of where a tool's files go, with the kinds and the platforms as
types. It exists so every tool here lands its files beside everybody else's,
whether or not renki launches it, and so a function that wants one root cannot
be handed another.

## The kinds

`Kind` is a sealed trait over four marker types. `Cache` is rebuilt on loss and
a cleanup may take it at any moment. `State` is what the tool wrote for itself
and would behave differently without. `Config` is what the person wrote.
`Data` is what the tool made for the person and cannot make again. Each kind
carries the XDG variable that names its directory, the default under the home
directory when that variable is unset, the macOS directory, and the tail a
kind takes under a macOS directory it shares with another. On macOS the
config, state and data roots all sit under `Application Support/<ns>`, state
and data one level down, and clearing the config root takes the other two
with it, which is the platform's layout and is documented on the kind.

## The platforms

`Platform` is a sealed trait over `Xdg`, for linux, the BSDs and illumos, and
`MacOs`. `Host` is a type alias picked by `cfg(target_os)`, and a target the
table has no column for refuses to build rather than borrowing the XDG one.
An exported XDG variable still wins on a mac, on the reasoning that whoever
set it said where their files go.

## The names

`Namespace` is the one path segment a tool owns under every root, checked by
a `const fn` so a tool's namespace is refused at compile time when it is
empty, holds a separator, or walks; `BadNamespace` says which. `Short` is the
short name a tool answers to and the prefix of its environment variables,
checked the same way to be something a shell can export a variable under;
`BadShort` says which. `EnvName<K>` is `<SHORT>_<KIND>` rendered through
`Display` with `Lower` and `LowerDisplay` doing the case work without
allocating, so the launcher and a tool that reads the table without it agree
on the variable's name.

## Resolution

`Sources` holds the three environment values a root depends on, read by the
caller: the tool's own variable, the kind's XDG variable, and `HOME`, each a
`Maybe` since an unset or non-text value is `Isnt`. `Root<K, P>::resolve`
takes a namespace and the sources and answers `Outcome<Root, NoHome<K>>`,
where `NoHome` is the one refusal: nothing named the directory and there is
no home to default under. The precedence is fixed and the same for every kind
and platform: the tool's own variable is the whole path, then the XDG
variable with the namespace under it, then the platform default. `Source`
says which of the three won and `Root::source` reports it, since the question
behind most configuration bugs is which of several places a value came from.
`Root::base` is the directory the namespace sits under, and `Display` prints
the whole path; that is the entire no-alloc surface, and a `std` caller
formats it into a `PathBuf`.

## Strings

Everything here borrows `&'a str` from the caller's environment text, and the
constants are `&'static str`. The stack's answer is `hilavitkutin_str::Str`
and arvo's types, and that is the long-term shape; this round keeps the
borrowed form, marks every site with a `lint:allow` carrying the reason and a
`FIXME:` naming the port, and leaves the port to a later unit. `Sealed` is the
private trait that closes `Kind` and `Platform`.
